# Shell scripts are LF, always. The bus is authored on a Windows host whose SYSTEM gitconfig
# sets core.autocrlf=true; Git for Windows' own bash tolerates the CR it leaves behind, so a
# CRLF checkout looks healthy right up until a WSL/Linux/macOS bash reads the same file and
# reports `\r: command not found`. Measured 2026-09-14 on the fleet doctrine bus, host
# VIRTUAL-TEN: CRLF confirmed on the shipped scripts in a fresh clone under core.autocrlf=true,
# and Git Bash ran them anyway -- which is what makes this silent.
#
# DELIBERATELY *.sh ONLY. An earlier draft also carried `*.py text eol=lf`; it was dropped
# because the measured CR hazard is bash, not python -- CPython accepts CRLF source on every
# platform -- while the *.py rule renormalises EVERY python file in this repository at its next
# checkout, which shows up as a repo-wide dirty tree in the other sessions that census this bus
# for uncommitted work. Scoping the rule to the files that actually break is the whole point.
*.sh text eol=lf
